# Code owners for the files that carry the guards (04-guards.md C2 and C4, point 22).
#
# Why: the override tag [ratchet: <name>] is written in a commit message, and the author of a pull request writes the commit
# messages. Nothing in the pipeline can tell a tag written for a real reason from one written to get past a guard. A review by
# a person who owns these files can. Every file the guards, the caps and the pipeline live in is listed here, and the file
# lists itself, so a pull request cannot drop its own review by editing this file.
#
# Copy to .github/CODEOWNERS (GitHub) or CODEOWNERS or .gitlab/CODEOWNERS (GitLab). Replace <owner> with one or more people or
# teams, never with a bot or an automation account (GitHub: @user, @org/team; GitLab: @user, @group, or [Section] blocks):
# the owner is the person who reads the reason of every override (09-reviewer-brief.md).
# The last matching rule wins, so a rule below must not name fewer owners than the rule above it.
# Bitbucket Cloud has no such file: default reviewers on the repository and the merge checks "minimum number of approvals"
# and "minimum number of default reviewers" do the same, and the profile names them (07-process.md section 12).
#
# Who can approve (read this before you enable the setting). On GitHub the author of a pull request cannot approve it (documented
# behaviour, UNVERIFIED on this repository). The kit's agents push and open pull requests with whatever credentials the session
# holds: when those are the owner's own, every pull request that touches a guard file is the owner's and no approval of the owner
# counts, so the pull request cannot merge without a bypass. Three states, in the order of how much of the rule they keep:
#   1. The agents work under a second identity (a machine user or a GitHub App) and the owner, a person, approves. The author may
#      be a bot; only the owner of the files may not be. This keeps the rule whole.
#   2. A second person is a code owner and approves. This keeps the rule whole.
#   3. A solo owner puts the owner's own account in the bypass list of a ruleset (or leaves administrators able to bypass). The
#      rule then stops every identity that has no bypass, and stops nothing when the agents use the owner's token: a merge by that
#      token is a bypass. Write that state in the profile as it is. The rule is not weakened by this paragraph; it is not met.
#   4. Unenforced: the host cannot enforce anything. A private repository on a free plan has no branch protection, no ruleset and
#      no enforced code owners (the API answered 403 "Upgrade to GitHub Pro" on the one repository read). This file stays, the
#      contract test (04-guards.md C4, point 22) still requires it and reads it, and the owner still reads the tag of every
#      override in git log; what is then not enforced is stated in the profile, in these words: nothing stops a merge without the
#      owner's review, nothing stops a pull request from editing this file or the workflow, and a merge waits on gh pr checks only
#      (07-process.md section 12). When the plan changes, the setting can be switched on and the file is already there.
# The ruleset option "Require approval of the most recent reviewable push" (the person who pushed last cannot approve) belongs to
# the same question; how it treats commits an agent pushed under the owner's name is UNVERIFIED.
#
# This file does nothing until the host enforces it, and that setting is not in the repository:
#   GitHub: Settings, Branches (or Rules, Rulesets), the rule of the main branch and of every other deploying branch, "Require a
#           pull request before merging" with the option that asks for review from code owners (the exact label differs between
#           branch protection and rulesets: UNVERIFIED, read it in the settings page), and no bypass for administrators (an admin
#           who can bypass can merge without the review).
#   GitLab: Settings, Repository, Protected branches, "Require approval from code owners" on the main branch and every other
#           deploying branch (a feature of paid tiers: where the plan lacks it, Settings, Merge requests, approval rules
#           with the owners as approvers and "Prevent editing approval rules in merge requests").
# A pull request that only touches other files is not held up: the rules below name the guard files only.

# The scripts: the ratchet script and its table, the dashboard, the checks script, kit-config.sh, the commit-message check,
# the audit gate, the caps file (scripts/debt-caps.json), the floors, prepush.sh.
/scripts/ <owner>

# The pipeline files of the host the project uses, and the workflow directory as a whole.
/.github/workflows/ <owner>
/.gitlab-ci.yml <owner>
/bitbucket-pipelines.yml <owner>

# The guards: the detector classes and the thin tests of the guard group (PHP: tests/Support/Architecture and the architecture
# tests of tests/Unit; another stack: the guard folder its tooling table names).
/tests/Support/Architecture/ <owner>
/tests/Unit/*ArchitectureTest.php <owner>

# The format commits that blame skips, the dependency bot's config (Renovate or Dependabot: keep the line of the one in use) and the
# move tool of the program.
/.git-blame-ignore-revs <owner>
/renovate.json <owner>
/.github/dependabot.yml <owner>
/tools/codemods/ <owner>

# This file itself.
/.github/CODEOWNERS <owner>
/CODEOWNERS <owner>
/.gitlab/CODEOWNERS <owner>
